# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy to .env.local and fill in.
#
# Nothing here is required to run the site in DEVELOPMENT. One thing is
# required in PRODUCTION: a lead transport. See "Lead delivery" in README.md.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Canonical site URL ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Canonical links, Open Graph URLs, the sitemap AND the consent-evidence source
# URL are all built from this one value.
#
# IT IS READ AT BUILD TIME, NOT AT RUN TIME. NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables are inlined
# by the compiler, so setting this when STARTING an already-built server has no
# effect — the value present during `npm run build` wins. On Vercel this is
# automatic. If you build an image once and configure it later (Docker, a CI
# artefact), the variable must be present in the BUILD environment.
#
# Unset, everything falls back to http://localhost:3000 and the production build
# prints a warning.
#
# IN PRODUCTION THIS IS EFFECTIVELY REQUIRED. A consent record's `sourceUrl` is
# derived from this value, and recording "http://localhost:3000/quote" as the
# page where a real person gave consent is a false statement in the one field
# whose entire purpose is provability. Rather than store that, a production
# build made without this variable REFUSES consent-bearing submissions with a
# 503 and logs the reason. Contact messages, which carry no consent block, keep
# working. See tests/regression/no-site-url.sh.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://example.com

# Search indexing. Left OFF deliberately: the site should not be indexed while
# it carries demonstration content or unfilled {{TOKENS}}.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ALLOW_INDEXING=1

# ── Lead delivery ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /api/lead reports success only when a real transport returns 2xx.
#
#   production, none configured  → 503   (never a false success)
#   production, all failed       → 502
#   development, none configured → 200 {"delivery":"simulated"} — nothing sent
#
# Set either transport, or both. A lead counts as delivered if any one succeeds.

# Email notification (Resend). All three are needed for the email transport.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# LEAD_FROM_EMAIL=              # must be on a domain verified with Resend
# LEAD_NOTIFY_EMAIL=
# AGENT_LEAD_NOTIFY_EMAIL=      # optional — producer applications only

# Persisted store — any endpoint that accepts a JSON POST: an Airtable
# automation, a Zapier/Make webhook, a Supabase edge function, a CRM intake URL.
# Email alone is not durable enough for a record that carries consent evidence.
# LEAD_STORE_URL=
# LEAD_STORE_TOKEN=             # optional — sent as "Authorization: Bearer …"

# ── Proxy model ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to 1 ONLY when every request reaches this app through a proxy that
# OVERWRITES the forwarding headers — Vercel, Cloudflare, an ALB, or an nginx
# you configured. On those platforms the leftmost X-Forwarded-For entry is the
# real client address and a visitor cannot spoof it.
#
# Leave it unset when the app is reachable directly (a bare `next start`, a
# container with a published port, local development). The headers are then
# used for COUNTING but never RECORDED: no IP appears in a consent record, and
# the reason why is stored beside the null so a reader is never left guessing.
#
# Counting happens either way. A caller who sends a malformed forwarding header,
# an empty one, or none at all still gets a bucket — being unidentifiable is not
# a way to opt out of rate limiting.
# TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=1

# ── Local production builds ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A production build whose NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL points at localhost or 127.0.0.1
# FAILS, because that value becomes the canonical URL, every sitemap entry and
# the source URL recorded as consent evidence. Set this to 1 only when a local
# production build is genuinely what you want — the test suite sets it.
# ALLOW_LOCAL_SITE_URL=1

# ── Rate limits (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /api/lead keeps TWO separate per-caller budgets in a 60-second window, so that
# cheap invalid traffic can never consume the capacity reserved for real leads.
#
#   LEAD_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IDENTITY  well-formed lead submissions per caller
#                                 (default 5). Charged only AFTER a request has
#                                 proved it is a valid lead.
#
#   LEAD_RATE_LIMIT_REJECTS       rejected requests per caller (default 30) —
#                                 415, 413, 400, 422, and honeypot hits. Deliberately looser: a
#                                 person fixing a validation error should never
#                                 reach it, while a script firing garbage
#                                 exhausts its own budget and nobody else's.
#
#   LEAD_RATE_LIMIT_GLOBAL        OFF by default (0). An optional site-wide
#                                 runaway guard, charged ONLY by valid
#                                 submissions. Leave it unset unless you have a
#                                 specific reason: a global ceiling that any
#                                 caller can exhaust is a denial-of-service
#                                 lever against everyone else, which is exactly
#                                 what an earlier version of this code shipped.
#
# All counters are in-process: per-instance and not durable. Fine for a
# prototype; move them to a shared store before relying on them.
# LEAD_RATE_LIMIT_PER_IDENTITY=5
# LEAD_RATE_LIMIT_REJECTS=30
# LEAD_RATE_LIMIT_GLOBAL=0

# ── Spam protection (Cloudflare Turnstile) ───────────────────────────────────
# ONE switch, not two. Set BOTH keys or NEITHER.
#
# A production build FAILS if exactly one is present, because the alternative
# is a deployment whose widget renders while the server verifies nothing.
# With neither set, the honeypot and the per-IP rate limiter still run.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
